# Code Quality & Working Standards

## Always read before writing

Before planning or writing any code, read:

1. The product docs — what the system must do. They are the source of truth;
   if code and docs disagree, the docs win and you ask. Start at
   `docs/product/business-rules.md` (general context and the list of
   projects), then read the project folder, e.g.
   `docs/product/sistema-de-pedidos/`.
2. `docs/architecture/project-standards.md` — folder structure, naming, the
   `apps/web` vs `apps/api` split, and the rule against creating business
   modules before their slice runs.
3. The current slice: `docs/history/slices/<id>-*.md` (`gam-13-…` with Linear,
   `1.1-…` without), and its Prompt section.

The development flow, the skills and the Linear/local modes are described in
`docs/workflow/`.

## Scope

- Implement only the slice you were asked for. Do not start the next one.
- Do not touch files outside the slice's file list without asking first.
- Do not refactor unrelated code, rename things, or "improve" what works.
- If a rule is ambiguous or missing, stop and ask. Never fill gaps with guesses.

## Code quality

- Match the existing pattern in the touched area. `apps/api/apps/core` and
  `apps/web/src/features/health` are the reference examples.
- The frontend never talks to the database; everything goes through the API.
- The API is the source of truth for validation and totals.
- Money is `Decimal` in Python and a decimal string in JSON. Never `float`.
- Keep `page.tsx` thin; logic lives in `features/` and `lib/`.
- Every business rule you implement gets a test. Edge cases listed in the slice
  are mandatory tests.
- Comments explain non-obvious intent or constraints, never restate the code.
- Code, identifiers and commit messages in English. Text shown in the UI, docs
  and README in Brazilian Portuguese.

## Documentation and Linear

- Plans live in `docs/history/plans/` and slices in `docs/history/slices/`,
  named `<id>-<title-kebab>.md` (lowercase, no accents). With Linear, a plan is
  a ticket and its slices are sub-issues (`gam-12-…`, `gam-13-…`); without
  Linear, plans are numbered and slices are `<plan>.<step>` (`1-…`, `1.1-…`).
  See `docs/workflow/modes.md`.
- Every folder under `docs/` has an index file (`README.md`; in
  `docs/product/`, `business-rules.md`). When you create a file there, add it
  to that index.
- History is not rewritten: plans get new "Histórico" lines, slices get new
  "Execuções" entries.
- Linear goes through the `linear` MCP server in `.cursor/mcp.json` only. Never
  write to Linear, change a status or invent an ID without the user's explicit
  confirmation. Rules: `docs/architecture/linear-mcp.md`.

## Done means

- `make test` passes (or `python manage.py test` and `npm test` without make).
- `make lint` is clean (ESLint + TypeScript).
- You did not commit. The human reads the diff, stages it and runs `/commit`
  (Conventional Commits starting with the ticket: `GAM-13 feat(api): …`).
  Never stage files yourself.

## Reporting

End every task with a short report a person can act on without reading the
code:

- **Files changed**, each with one line on why.
- **Tests run** and their result (the actual command and pass/fail count).
- **Rules covered**: which business rules and edge cases (E1…E17) now have tests.
- **Open questions** or decisions you had to make, in plain language.
